<header class="topbar">
  <div class="shell topbar-in">
    <a class="brand" href="{{ site.home_path }}">{{ site.name }}</a>
    {%- comment -%}
      The mobile menu is a checkbox and a label: the one disclosure pattern
      that needs no JavaScript and can still be forced open by CSS at wide
      viewports. A <details> element cannot be, so the desktop navigation
      would vanish with it.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <input class="menu-toggle" type="checkbox" id="menu-toggle" aria-label="{{ t.menu }}">
    <label class="menu-button" for="menu-toggle" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></label>
    <nav class="menu-nav" aria-label="{{ t.nav }}">
      {% for item in site.nav %}<a href="{{ item.href }}"{% if item.active %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>{{ item.label }}</a>{% endfor %}
      {%- comment -%}
        Each language is named in its own words, because a reader looking for
        their language scans for its own name. A <details> disclosure is a
        real dropdown with no script, and it scales past two languages.

        The closed control carries the current language twice, as its name
        and as its code: where the bar is short of room the stylesheet shows
        the code, and the names are still what the open list offers.

        What it is — "Language" — is text inside the control and not an
        aria-label: a label would replace the visible words in the control's
        accessible name, and a reader of the screen would then hear the same
        name on every page, whatever the language.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {% if page.alternates.size > 1 %}
      <details class="langs">
        <summary><span class="visually-hidden">{{ t.language }}: </span>{% for alt in page.alternates %}{% if alt.locale == site.locale %}<span class="lang-name">{{ alt.name }}</span><span class="lang-code">{{ alt.locale }}</span>{% endif %}{% endfor %}</summary>
        <ul>
          {% for alt in page.alternates %}
          <li><a href="{{ alt.href }}" hreflang="{{ alt.locale }}" lang="{{ alt.locale }}"{% if alt.locale == site.locale %} aria-current="true"{% endif %}>{{ alt.name }}</a></li>
          {% endfor %}
        </ul>
      </details>
      {% endif %}
      {%- comment -%}
        The short wording: this button shares one line with the whole
        navigation, in every language.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {% if theme.options.quote_href != blank %}<a class="btn btn-solid" href="{{ theme.options.quote_href }}">{{ t.quote_short }}</a>{% endif %}
    </nav>
  </div>
</header>
